// Where the loop variable an 'each' declares can and cannot be seen.
//
// The parser puts the variable in a block of its own that wraps the loop, so
// its scope starts after the range's lower bound and ends with the loop. Both
// edges of that scope are asserted here, and both fail at name resolution
// rather than at parse, so they cannot share a file with each-parse.cone.

// The scope ends with the loop, so the name is gone on the next statement.
fn afterTheLoop() {
  each i in 0 < 5 {
  }
  imm n = i              //~ ErrorUnkName:11 "does not refer to a declared name"
}

// It does not begin until the variable is declared, and the range's lower bound
// is that declaration's initial value, so a bound naming the loop variable does
// not see it. The upper bound is the loop's condition and would see it; the
// lower bound never can.
fn inItsOwnBound() {
  each i in i < 5 {      //~ ErrorUnkName:13 "does not refer to a declared name"
  }
}
